IMPORT prelude

-- Negation as failure (NAF), the functional way.
-- A MAYBE BOOLEAN is a proposition that may or may not have been settled:
--   JUST TRUE   proven true
--   JUST FALSE  proven false
--   NOTHING     no proof either way (the open question / the default)
-- Negation as failure is the closed-world assumption -- the act of defaulting
-- "unproven" to FALSE -- and that act is already `fromMaybe FALSE`.

§ `Negation as Failure`

-- `holds` grounds a MAYBE BOOLEAN under the closed-world assumption: absence
-- of proof is FALSE. This is exactly `fromMaybe FALSE`; the legal reading
-- earns it a name of its own.
GIVEN p IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
holds p @nlg %p% has been proven true
  MEANS fromMaybe FALSE p

-- Negation as failure: succeeds exactly when p cannot be proven true. Both
-- the refuted case (JUST FALSE) and the unknown case (NOTHING) make `naf`
-- succeed, mirroring Prolog's `\+` under SLDNF resolution.
GIVEN p IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
naf p @nlg %p% has not been proven true
  MEANS NOT (holds p)

-- The open-world dual of `holds`: an undecided proposition defaults to TRUE
-- ("not forbidden => permitted"). Flipping the default FALSE <-> TRUE is
-- exactly the closed-world / open-world switch.
GIVEN p IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
presumed p @nlg %p% has not been proven false
  MEANS fromMaybe TRUE p
